## 4. Committing

- **Commit endpoint:** `http://127.0.0.1:7351/git_commit` (the tool server
  `claude-agent.yolo` names; `GET http://127.0.0.1:7351/health` reports
  `"repo_root": "C:\\GitHub\\Berreman"` when it serves this repo).
- **Payload:** as `common.manual-system-md` §7 —
  `{"files": [...], "message": "..."}`. No repo-specific extra field.
- **Response:** HTTP 200 with a `sha` is success. HTTP 400 with an `error` is a
  rejection and the body says why.
- **Subject convention:** `<NNNN>-manual`, the spec folder's number. Arc commits
  use `<NNNN>-arc-slice: …` and the spec writer `<NNNN>-spec-cycle: …`; do not
  imitate those from a manual batch.
- **How to tell the tool is down:** the POST fails to connect at all, or
  `/health` names another repository. Only one repo can commit at a time, so that
  is normal. Escalate and stop; do not shell out to `git commit`.

Two traps:

- **The file list is not a sandbox.** The tool commits every dirty path in the
  tree even when you pass an explicit list. Run `git status --porcelain` first.
  Unrelated dirt is a legitimate stop.
- **Past roughly 600 dirty paths the endpoint returns a bare HTTP 500** (the
  host's argument-length limit). Escalate and say how many paths were dirty.

`.tool-server-data/` is gitignored and untracked. Never read it, never pass it to
the commit tool.

